---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Ladder from '../../components/Ladder.astro';
import { bip85 } from '../../data/ladder.js';
---
<Base
  title="The self-custody configuration ladder · BitcoinKeys.guide"
  description="Every serious Bitcoin self-custody setup fits on one ladder of rising complexity — from single-signature to collaborative multisig. Find the lowest rung that covers you, and stop there."
  verified="2026-07-15"
>
  <section class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">The spine of the guide</p>
    <h1>The configuration <span class="accent">ladder</span></h1>
    <p class="lead">The major self-custody setups arrange themselves into a ladder of rising complexity. Each rung solves a real problem — and introduces new ways to fail.</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="label">How to use it</span>
      <p>Don’t ask “what’s the most secure setup?” Ask “<strong>what’s the simplest setup that adequately covers the risks I can actually name?</strong>” Find that rung, build it, test it — and don’t climb higher until a real reason pushes you up. Complexity you don’t fully control is itself a threat.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="sub">New to keys, seed words, and what “signing” means? <a href="/how-it-works">See how this all actually works →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="config-head"><span class="config-eyebrow">Wallet configuration</span><span class="config-sub">how your keys are arranged — the ladder proper</span></p>
    <Ladder />
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Reading the ladder</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Most newcomers</strong> belong on rung 1 (single-signature) and should stay there through their first year.</li>
      <li><strong>Most substantial holders</strong> settle at rung 3 or 4 (self-run or collaborative multi-signature).</li>
      <li><strong>Bigger multi-signature (3-of-5)</strong> is right for a small minority — large holdings or genuine multi-party needs. It’s the same technology as 2-of-3, so it lives on the <a href="/ladder/multisig">multi-signature rung</a> rather than as a separate step.</li>
      <li>Jumping straight to the top introduces complexity you’re not yet equipped to manage. That complexity is a bigger risk than the ones it’s meant to solve.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="label">Optional · Advanced backup</span>
      <p><strong>Shamir backup</strong> (SLIP-39) is a way to split a single seed’s backup into shares — any few rebuild it, no one share reveals anything. It’s a <em>backup method</em>, not a signing configuration, so it sits beside the ladder rather than on it. <a href="/ladder/shamir">How Shamir backup works →</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout warn">
      <span class="label">{bip85.name}</span>
      <p set:html={bip85.note.replace('BIP-85', '<strong>BIP-85</strong>')}></p>
    </div>

  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="label">Which rung is yours?</span>
      <p>This page teaches the ladder; it doesn’t place you on it. The <a href="/quiz">2-minute quiz</a> does that — six plain questions, and it names the rung that fits what you’re actually protecting against. It errs toward the <em>lowest</em> rung that covers you, because that’s the rule this whole ladder is built on.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href="/quiz" class="btn btn-primary">Find my rung <span class="arw">→</span></a>
      <a href="/how-to" class="btn btn-ghost">Learn the hands-on skills first</a>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>


<style>
  section { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .config-head { display: flex; align-items: baseline; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem 0.8rem; margin: 0 0 0.9rem; }
  .config-eyebrow { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.74rem; letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }
  .config-sub { color: var(--faint); font-size: 0.86rem; }
  .cta-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.7rem; margin-top: 1.4rem; }
</style>

